standardize — z-scores                                                 data · tier 1 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    standardize(x)

DESCRIPTION
    Each value less the mean, divided by the standard deviation, so that the result has mean zero and
    standard deviation one: `(x - mean(x)) / sd(x)`, with the sample standard deviation (divisor n − 1) that
    `sd` gives. Both are taken over the values present, each with the reducers' warning for the rows with
    `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), and those rows stay `NA`. A column with no spread,
    one value or all values equal, has no z-scores: every row is `NA` with the warning `0 / 0` gives
    (W0205), never an error, so a grouped summary over small groups still prints. Like R's `scale` and
    Stata's `egen std()`.

ARGUMENTS
    x           Column<Float>. The column to standardise.

VALUE
    A `Column<Float>` with mean zero and standard deviation one.

DETAILS
    The sample standard deviation with divisor n − 1, as R's `scale` and `sd` use; standardising by the
    population deviation is `center(x) / sqrt(mean(center(x)^2))`, not offered.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Z0214   a list holds values of more than one type
    Z0217   the lists have different lengths
    Z0219   a list has no value, or none but NA, so its type is unknown
    Z0401   a definition is not a list, or a list holds a value a column cannot (a list, a record, a frame)
    Z0406   no column is defined
    W0201   missing values skipped, with the count; all missing, or no rows at all, gives NA
    W0204   Inf and -Inf both present, so the result is NA
    Z0101   no such variable in the frame
    Z0102   a variable that differs only in case from the name written
    W0205   one value only, an infinite value, or squared deviations that overflow, so the result is NA

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    R Core Team, the `scale` function of the base package.
    Stata, `egen` with the `std()` function.

SEE ALSO
    center, mean, sd

EXAMPLES
    > let d = frame(x = [2, 4, 9])
    > standardize(d["x"])
    Column<Float> · 3 values
    -0.83205
    -0.27735
    1.1094
    > frame(z = standardize(d["x"])).sd(z)
    1

`standardize` arrives with a release after 1.0, in the `learn` package: z-scores with the analysis-set mean and sd
